From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shelley Vohr <shelley.vohr@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 11:27:27 +0200
Subject: unix,process: never shrink the stdio socket pair buffers (#5230)

Commit 78940725 ("macos: increase child process stdio buffer size") set
SO_SNDBUF and SO_RCVBUF to 64 KiB on the socket pairs created for
UV_CREATE_PIPE stdio, to lift macOS' 8 KiB default. It did so
unconditionally though, and on Linux, where AF_UNIX stream sockets get
net.core.wmem_default (212992 bytes unless tuned), that shrinks the
buffers to 128 KiB and spends four setsockopt() calls per pipe on it.

Read the current size first and only ever raise it. macOS and the BSDs
(8 KiB defaults) get exactly what they got before; Linux keeps its
larger default and trades the four setsockopt() calls for four cheaper
getsockopt() calls.

The smaller buffer mostly shows when parent and child exchange large
messages over stdio, i.e. a bulk write phase immediately followed by
reading a bulk reply (language servers, MCP-style tool servers,
formatters fed through stdin). Round trip of a length-prefixed message
echoed by a plain read()/write() child, parent using uv_write() and
64 KiB reads, 30 interleaved runs per side on Linux 6.12 / x86-64:

  256 KiB: 160us -> 112us   1 MiB: 456us -> 305us   4 MiB: 1120us -> 984us
  (p50; all p < 1e-30; parent CPU time -31%/-24%/-7%)

64 KiB messages, one-directional bulk transfers and uv_spawn() latency
itself are unchanged within noise. Through Node.js' child_process (same
libuv in deps/uv) the 1 MiB exchange improves by 8% (p < 1e-5).

The new spawn_stdio_socket_buffer_size test checks both halves of the
contract: at least 64 KiB everywhere, and never less than what a fresh
socketpair() gets on the running system.

diff --git a/deps/uv/src/unix/process.c b/deps/uv/src/unix/process.c
index b87e1f40495f192235b9d36bc0dc3c6992473981..8f8703595dffb882ca19e736ac80b1921668909c 100644
--- a/deps/uv/src/unix/process.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/unix/process.c
@@ -205,6 +205,23 @@ void uv__wait_children(uv_loop_t* loop) {
  * avoided. Since this isn't called on those targets, the function
  * doesn't even need to be defined for them.
  */
+/* Raise a socket buffer to at least `size` bytes but never shrink it: the
+ * defaults differ wildly between platforms (8 KiB on macOS, ~208 KiB on Linux)
+ * and a smaller buffer means more wake-ups per bulk transfer over stdio.
+ */
+static void uv__process_stdio_bufsize(int fd, int opt, int size) {
+  socklen_t len;
+  int cur;
+
+  len = sizeof(cur);
+  if (getsockopt(fd, SOL_SOCKET, opt, &cur, &len))
+    cur = 0;
+
+  if (cur < size)
+    setsockopt(fd, SOL_SOCKET, opt, &size, sizeof(size));
+}
+
+
 static int uv__process_init_stdio(uv_stdio_container_t* container, int fds[2]) {
   int mask;
   int fd;
@@ -228,8 +245,8 @@ static int uv__process_init_stdio(uv_stdio_container_t* container, int fds[2]) {
 
       if (ret == 0)
         for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
-          setsockopt(fds[i], SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVBUF, &size, sizeof(size));
-          setsockopt(fds[i], SOL_SOCKET, SO_SNDBUF, &size, sizeof(size));
+          uv__process_stdio_bufsize(fds[i], SO_RCVBUF, size);
+          uv__process_stdio_bufsize(fds[i], SO_SNDBUF, size);
         }
     }
 
